    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=You are reviewing news articles gathered while tracking AI video generation\ncompetitors. Some are duplicate coverage of the same event from different\noutlets. Some mention multiple competitors in one roundup piece. Some\naren't really news about any tracked competitor at all, despite matching\nthe search.\n\nArticles:\n{{ JSON.stringify($json.articles) }}\n\nSTEP 1 — Group into distinct real-world stories.\n\nTwo articles are the SAME story only if they describe the same specific\nevent or announcement (e.g., the same funding round, the same launch).\nSimilar topic or genre is NOT enough — do not merge separate comparison\narticles or separate listicles just because they cover similar ground.\n\nExample — SAME story: \"Kling 3.0 raises $2.8B\" + \"Kling captures majority\nof July funding\" (same funding round, different outlets).\nExample — DIFFERENT stories: \"Top 10 AI Video Tools\" listicle + \"Seedance\nvs Kling\" comparison article (different pieces, just similar topic, even\nif both mention the same competitors).\n\nFor each story, list every tracked competitor it's genuinely about.\n\nSTEP 2 — Judge relevance for each story.\n\nMark relevant: true if the article is substantively about the tracked\ncompetitor itself — its product, technology, business, funding,\npartnerships, or strategy. This includes factual descriptions, reviews,\nand comparisons of the product, not just breaking-news events.\n\nMark relevant: false if any of the following apply:\n- The competitor's name isn't actually mentioned (being topically about\n  AI video in general is not enough on its own).\n- The article is really about something else — a third party's content,\n  project, or story — that merely happens to use or mention the\n  competitor's tool in passing, rather than being about the competitor\n  itself. Example: a meme video \"made with\" a competitor's tool is about\n  the meme, not the competitor — mark false.\n- The article is a generic listicle or roundup that mentions the\n  competitor's name only as one entry among many, with no real\n  substance about that competitor specifically.\n\nOne exception: a personnel move (an executive or employee leaving the\ncompetitor) IS relevant, even though the article's nominal subject is\ntheir new venture — it signals something real about the competitor's\ntalent and strategy. Mark these true.\n\nReturn only a JSON object with a \"stories\" key. For each story, include\nevery field (url, title, description, source, date) for every article\nin that story, not just the URL — the next step needs this content to\nwrite from.",
        "hasOutputParser": true,
        "batching": {}
      },
